@page Learning goals
@label Chapter 5 · Before you begin
@plane none

In the first four chapters you met the module's three technologies one at a time: hardware that senses and acts, networks that carry data, and behaviourist AI that learns from feedback. In real life they almost always work together. This chapter follows them into homes, hospitals, roads, factories and classrooms, shows how they combine into one working system, and looks at what can go wrong when they do.

@goals light | Learning goals
You will:

- Discover how hardware, networks and behaviourist AI are used in real-world applications
- Understand how physical devices, communication systems and intelligent algorithms work together
- Explain how AI systems respond to feedback and adapt their behaviour to achieve specific goals
- Recognise the opportunities and challenges of integrating these technologies into everyday applications
@end

@box dark | Your route through the chapter
| Subtopic | The question it answers |
|---|---|
| 5.1 Applications of Hardware, Networks and Behaviorist AI in Real-Life Situations | How do the three technologies work together as one system, and how fast must it respond? |
| 5.2 Smart Homes | How do connected devices sense, automate and learn at home? |
| 5.3 Healthcare | How do wearables and intelligent systems watch over patients? |
| 5.4 Transportation | How do traffic systems and vehicles sense, connect and drive themselves? |
| 5.5 Industry & Manufacturing | How do robots and smart factories work, and how can a robot learn from rewards? |
| 5.6 Education | How do smart devices and adaptive systems help people learn? |
| 5.7 Conclusion | How do the three technologies shape everyday life and future innovation? |
@end



@page Key terms
@label Chapter 5 · Before you begin
@plane none

@terms light | Key terms
Smart system: A system that combines hardware, networks and intelligent software to perform tasks: sensing, deciding and acting with little human help.
Sensor: A device that measures a physical quantity, such as temperature, light or movement, and turns it into a signal a computer can process.
Actuator: A device that turns an electrical signal into physical action, e.g. a motor turning, a valve opening or a heater switching on.
Internet of Things (IoT): Everyday objects fitted with sensors and network connections so that they can share data and be controlled remotely.
Feedback loop: A cycle in which the result of an action is measured and used to choose the next action.
Behaviourist AI: An approach to AI in which behaviour is shaped by interaction with the environment, through fixed stimulus–response rules or by learning from rewards and penalties.
Reactive control: Behaviour produced by fixed stimulus–response rules that do not change with experience.
Reinforcement learning (RL): Learning by trial and error: an agent tries actions and learns which ones earn the most reward over time.
Reward: A positive feedback score telling an agent that an action had a good result.
Penalty: A negative reward telling an agent that an action had a bad result.
Policy: The strategy an agent uses to choose an action in each situation (state).
Real-time processing: Processing data immediately or with very little delay, so that the result arrives before a strict deadline.
Batch processing: Collecting data and processing it all together later, when a delay causes no harm.
Latency: The delay between an event, or a message being sent, and the response; usually measured in milliseconds (ms).
Edge computing: Processing data on or near the device that collected it, rather than on a distant server.
Cloud computing: Storing and processing data on remote servers reached over the internet.
Automation: The use of technology to perform tasks with minimal human involvement.
Wearable device: A small computer worn on the body, such as a smartwatch, that uses sensors to track activity and health.
Remote patient monitoring: Collecting a patient's health readings at home and sending them over a network for doctors and nurses to check.
Connected vehicle: A vehicle that exchanges data with other vehicles (V2V) and with roadside equipment such as traffic lights (V2I).
Autonomous vehicle: A vehicle that uses sensors, onboard computers and actuators to drive itself with little or no human input.
Predictive maintenance: Using sensor data to predict when a machine is likely to fail, so that it can be repaired before it breaks.
Smart factory: A factory whose machines, robots and sensors are networked so that production can be monitored and adjusted automatically.
Cobot: A collaborative robot designed to work safely alongside people.
Adaptive learning: Educational software that changes the difficulty and content for each learner based on their answers.
Human-AI interaction (HAI): The ways humans communicate and interact with AI-powered systems.
Black box: An AI system whose internal decision-making cannot easily be understood, even by its developers.
Ethical AI: The development and use of AI in ways that are fair, responsible and transparent.
Smart city: A city that uses connected sensors, networks and AI to manage services such as traffic, energy, water and waste.
@end



@page Where the three technologies meet
@section 5.1 Applications of Hardware, Networks and Behaviorist AI in Real-Life Situations

In Chapters 1–4 you studied hardware, networks and behaviourist AI separately, but in the real world you rarely meet them alone. Think about a robot vacuum cleaner. Its bump and cliff sensors detect obstacles and stairs (hardware). It reports its progress to an app over Wi-Fi (network). Its software chooses what to do when it hits a chair leg (behaviourist AI), and its wheel motors carry out that choice (hardware again). A machine that combines the three technologies in this way is called a **smart system**.

@box light w=90 | What makes a system "smart"?
A **smart system** combines hardware, networks and intelligent software so that a machine can sense its surroundings, share information, decide what to do and act, often within milliseconds and with little or no human help. Thermostats, watches, cars, factories and whole cities all contain smart systems, and every one has the same four parts:

- **Sensors** measure the physical world: temperature, light, movement, distance.
- A **network** carries readings to wherever the deciding happens, and carries commands back.
- A **processor** running software or AI decides what to do.
- **Actuators** turn the decision into physical action: a motor turns, a valve opens.
@end

@box dark w=94 right | Analogy: a smart system works like a body
