Like a body, a smart system senses, passes signals along, decides and moves. The analogy describes the *roles* only: the machine does not think or feel like a person.
@end

@try
Choose one electronic device in your home, such as a smart thermostat, a robot vacuum cleaner or a smartphone. Identify a) the sensors it uses to gather real-world data, b) how it communicates across a network, c) the actuator or output action it performs.
@end



@page Sense, connect, decide, act

To see the four parts working together, follow one example from start to finish. On a large farm, watering on a fixed timer wastes water on wet days and leaves crops thirsty on hot ones. A smart irrigation system waters each zone only when it needs it.

@box light | One trip around the loop on a smart farm
| Stage | What happens |
|---|---|
| 1 · Sense | soil-moisture **sensors** measure how wet the ground is in every zone |
| 2 · Connect | a long-range, low-power **network** (LoRaWAN) carries the readings to the farm's computer |
| 3 · Decide | **software or AI** combines the readings with the forecast to decide each zone's water |
| 4 · Act | solenoid valves (**actuators**) open Zone A's drip lines for 12 minutes; Zone B stays closed |
@end

@cycle Sense > Connect > Decide > Act

Acting changes the environment, so tomorrow's readings show whether 12 minutes was the right amount, and the cycle repeats. A cycle in which the result of each action is measured and used to choose the next one is called a **feedback loop**. The *decide* step can work in two quite different ways:

@row
@box alt | A fixed rule
A programmer writes: *if moisture is below 30%, open the valve for 10 minutes.* The rule is simple, predictable and easy to check, but it waters even when rain is forecast, and it never improves.
@end
@box alt | A learned policy
A **reward** for healthy crops and a small **penalty** for every litre used teach it, over many weeks, to grow the same harvest with less water. It needs data and testing before it can be trusted.
@end
@endrow

Being connected does not make a system learn: it only *learns* when feedback about its results changes what it does next time. A fixed rule is **reactive control**; a policy improved by rewards and penalties is **reinforcement learning** (Chapter 2).

@try
For the smart farm, give one advantage of a learned watering policy over a fixed rule, and one reason a farmer might still prefer the fixed rule.
@end



@page Deciding in time

Some computer jobs can wait. A bank can work out every customer's monthly statement overnight in one large batch, and nobody is harmed if it takes an hour: this is **batch processing**. A smart system that controls something physical is different, because its answer is only useful if it arrives in time. **Real-time processing** means processing data immediately, or within a strict time limit called a **deadline**. Real-time does not simply mean "fast": it means *fast enough, every time*.

@box dark | How strict is the deadline?
| Type | If the deadline is missed … | Examples |
|---|---|---|
| Hard real-time | the system fails; people or equipment may be harmed | car airbag, anti-lock brakes |
| Soft real-time | quality drops, but nothing is damaged | video call, online game |
| Not real-time (batch) | the result simply arrives later | bank statements, overnight back-ups |
@end

Every stage of the feedback loop takes time: the sensor must measure, the network must carry the data, the processor must compute and the actuator must move. The delay added by the network is called **latency**. It is usually measured in milliseconds (ms); one millisecond is a thousandth of a second.

@box light | Worked example: adding up the delays
A safety system must stop a robot arm within **100 ms** of a person stepping too close. Each stage of the loop adds delay:
@flow Sense :: 2 ms > Send :: 20 ms > Decide :: 10 ms > Act :: 50 ms
Total: 2 + 20 + 10 + 50 = **82 ms**, so the deadline is met with 18 ms to spare. If a busy network raised the latency from 20 ms to 45 ms, the total would become 107 ms and this hard deadline would be missed. That is why engineers keep slow network links out of safety-critical loops wherever they can.
@end

@try
Classify each application as hard real-time, soft real-time or not real-time, and justify one of your answers: a) an aircraft's fly-by-wire control system, b) streaming a film, c) a heart pacemaker, d) processing weekly payroll.
@end



@page Edge or cloud?

Where should the deciding happen? In **cloud computing**, data travels over the internet to a powerful remote data centre and the answer travels back, which adds latency and makes the system depend on its connection. In **edge computing**, a processor on the device itself, or very close to it, does the work; chips such as GPUs and NPUs (Chapter 3) let small devices run AI quickly.

@box light | Comparing cloud and edge
| | Cloud computing | Edge computing |
|---|---|---|
| Where it happens | remote data centres, often far away | on the device or a nearby gateway |
| Typical network delay | about 50–200 ms | about 1–10 ms |
| Data sent | a lot: continuous raw readings | a little: results and summaries |
| If the link fails | the system stops | it keeps working locally |
| Best for | heavy work that can wait, e.g. training AI | split-second decisions, e.g. braking |
@end

Edge and cloud are partners, not rivals. A campus delivery robot steers round people using its own computer, because that cannot wait. Over the network it receives delivery jobs, uploads logs that engineers use to retrain its AI, and downloads improved, tested policies.

@box dark | What the network carries
Every message needs context: which device sent it, when (a **timestamp**) and in what order (a **sequence number**), so the robot can reject a command that is too old or already carried out. Context matters for learning too: if the report on action 42 arrives after the one on action 43, sequence numbers match each result to the right action. A lost report means the result is unknown, not a reward of zero.
@end

@video Watch the tutorial: Networking for behaviorist AI (Video 04) | https://github.com/user-github-me/cs-ai-project-01/blob/main/assets/tutorials/stage2/videos/BlueImpulse_Video04_Networking_for_Behaviorist_AI.mp4

@try
A delivery robot must a) stop for a pedestrian, b) plan tomorrow's routes for the fleet, c) retrain its policy on a week of logs. Should each job run on the robot (edge) or in the cloud? Justify one answer.
@end



@page The connected home
@section 5.2 Smart Homes

A **smart home** is a home whose everyday devices, such as lights, heating, locks, cameras and speakers, are networked so that they can be controlled from an app, by voice or automatically. Each device is a small smart system, and together they form part of the **Internet of Things (IoT)**.

@row
@image 5.1 smart system.PNG | One hub links the home's lights, cameras, heating and locks.
Devices connect in different ways. A camera streams video, so it needs the high data rate of **Wi-Fi**. A door sensor sends a few bytes and must run for a year on a small battery, so it uses a low-power network such as **Zigbee** or **Thread**, in which mains-powered devices pass messages on to form a **mesh** that reaches every room. A **hub**, or the router itself, links everything to the internet.
@endrow

@flow Door sensor :: the door opens > Hub :: Zigbee to Wi-Fi > Cloud server :: runs your routine > Phone app :: "Front door opened"

@box light | The home's senses
| Sensor | What it detects | Example use |
|---|---|---|
| Temperature | how warm the air is | heating control |
| Passive infrared (PIR) | body heat from a moving person | lights on as you walk in; alarm |
| Magnetic contact | a door or window opening | security alert |
| Smoke / carbon monoxide | smoke particles or CO gas | alarm and phone alert |
| Microphone | sound | hears a voice assistant's wake word |
@end

On the output side, **actuators** do the work: a **relay** (an electrically controlled switch) turns the boiler or a lamp on and off, **motors** move a lock's bolt or the blinds, and a **speaker** sounds an alarm.

@try
A smart video doorbell has a camera, a PIR motion sensor, a speaker and a Wi-Fi chip. Classify each part as a sensor, an actuator (output) or a network component, and say what it does when a visitor arrives.
@end



@page Homes that act on their own

Connecting devices lets you control them from anywhere, but a home only becomes truly smart when it can act without being told. Smart-home automation comes in three levels, and only the last one learns:

@stack Remote control :: you tap the app and the light comes on > Fixed routine :: IF motion is detected after sunset THEN switch on the hall light > Learning automation :: the system notices your habits and adjusts its own behaviour

A **fixed routine** is the reactive control of Chapter 2: the same stimulus always triggers the same response. Many robot vacuum cleaners work this way: when the bumper is pressed they reverse and turn, when the cliff sensor finds no floor they back away from the stairs, and when the battery is low they follow the dock's infrared signal home. The rules never change, yet together they clean the room.

@box light | Worked example: a learning thermostat
A learning thermostat treats every change you make as **feedback**. From Monday to Thursday you turn the heating up from 17 °C to 21 °C at about 06:30, and each time it records the day, time and setting. By Friday it has spotted the pattern and starts heating at 06:00 by itself, so the house is already 21 °C when you get up. If you turn it down again, that correction works like a **penalty** for its last choice and the schedule shifts; a day with no corrections suggests it got things right.
@end

@box dark | The cost of connection
Every connected device is a possible way in for an attacker. In 2016 the **Mirai** malware took over hundreds of thousands of cameras and routers that still used their factory passwords and used them in a DDoS attack that made websites such as Twitter and Netflix hard to reach for hours. Smart speakers and cameras also collect private data. To protect a smart home, change default passwords, install updates, use encryption and choose devices that collect only the data they need.
@end

@try
Explain how a learning thermostat uses feedback from the people in a home to change its behaviour. Then give one security risk of connecting it to the internet and one way to reduce that risk.
@end



@page Wearables and patient monitoring
@section 5.3 Healthcare

Healthcare is one of the most valuable places to use smart systems, and one of the most sensitive: the environment being sensed is a human body, the data is private and a wrong decision can harm someone. So most systems *support* doctors and nurses rather than replace them.

@box light | What a smartwatch senses
A **wearable device** is a small computer worn on the body, such as a smartwatch, a fitness band or a skin patch.

- **Optical heart-rate sensor:** green LEDs shine into the skin and a light sensor measures how much light comes back. Blood absorbs green light, so each heartbeat changes the reading.
- **Accelerometer and gyroscope:** measure movement to count steps, track sleep and detect a hard fall; some watches then call the emergency services if the wearer does not respond.
- **ECG sensor:** on some watches, records the heart's electrical signal to check its rhythm.
@end

The watch processes its raw readings itself (edge computing) and sends only summaries, which saves battery power and network bandwidth:

@flow Watch :: measures and summarises > Bluetooth :: to the phone > Mobile network :: encrypted upload > Clinic dashboard :: a nurse reviews

@box dark | Remote patient monitoring
People with long-term conditions can be monitored at home. A heart-failure patient might weigh themselves each morning on a connected scale. A gain of about 2 kg in three days can mean fluid is building up, so the system flags the patient and a nurse phones that day, instead of the patient arriving at hospital weeks later as an emergency.
@end

In hospital, bedside monitors send heart rate, blood pressure, oxygen level and breathing rate over the ward network to the nurses' station. Fixed limits, such as *alarm if the heart rate is above 120 beats per minute*, cause so many false alarms that staff can tune them out (**alarm fatigue**), so smarter systems combine several readings and trends to raise fewer, more meaningful alerts.

@try
Trace one heart-rate reading from a patient's smartwatch to a nurse's screen, naming the hardware or network used at each step. Then explain one benefit of processing the readings on the watch.
@end



@page Intelligent health systems

Monitoring shows what is happening; **intelligent health systems** use AI to decide what should happen next, either acting automatically in a feedback loop or advising a doctor.

@box alt | A feedback loop in the body: the artificial pancreas
People with type 1 diabetes must take insulin to control the sugar (glucose) in their blood. A **hybrid closed-loop system** does much of this automatically:
@flow Glucose sensor :: every few minutes > Bluetooth :: sends the reading > Algorithm :: decides the dose > Insulin pump :: delivers it
Each reading shows how the body responded to the last dose, and this feedback lets the algorithm learn the person's insulin needs. The user still enters meals, so the system is called *hybrid*.
@end

@box dark | AI in the emergency room
@image 5.7 reader activity emergency room.PNG w=38 | Emergency departments are busy day and night.
A busy emergency department can take hundreds of chest X-rays a day. An AI system trained on thousands of earlier images can check each one in seconds, mark areas that look abnormal and move urgent cases to the top of the doctor's list. The doctor still makes the diagnosis; the AI adds a fast, tireless extra check. But it only recognises patterns like those in its training data.

This kind of AI learns from labelled examples rather than from rewards: not every AI is behaviourist.
@clear
@end

Many AI systems are **black boxes**: their outputs come from millions of calculations that even their developers cannot easily explain. If an alert just says *High risk*, a doctor may trust it blindly (**over-reliance**) or ignore it (**under-reliance**). A transparent system shows its evidence: the area that triggered the alert and how confident it is.

@try
An AI system scans chest X-rays for signs of lung illness in a busy hospital emergency room. a) Identify two major efficiency advantages of using it. b) Identify one potential risk if the AI meets an unusually rare chest deformity that it has never seen before.
@end



@page Smart traffic, connected cars
@section 5.4 Transportation

Roads are a shared system: thousands of vehicles, traffic lights and people all affect one another. Smart transport uses sensors and networks to see the whole picture, and AI to keep traffic moving safely.

@h2 Smart traffic systems

A traditional traffic light runs on a **fixed timer**, even at 3 a.m. when the roads are empty. An **adaptive traffic signal** changes its timing to match the traffic: wire loops buried in the road detect waiting cars, cameras and radar measure queues, and fibre or mobile networks carry the readings to a controller that sets each green light.

@box light | Worked example: sharing out the green light
At a junction with a 60-second cycle, a fixed timer gives each road 30 seconds of green (ignoring the amber phases). At 08:00, cameras count 18 cars queuing on the main road and 6 on the side road. Sharing the 60 seconds in proportion to the queues gives the main road 18 ÷ 24 × 60 = **45 seconds** and the side road 6 ÷ 24 × 60 = **15 seconds**, so the long queue clears and nobody waits at a red light beside an empty road.
@end

Researchers also train traffic signals with **reinforcement learning**. The **state** is the length of the queue on each road, the **action** is which road gets the next green light, and the **reward** is the total waiting time made negative, so shorter queues earn a higher reward. Over thousands of simulated rush hours, the controller learns timings that keep queues shorter than a fixed timer can.

@h2 Connected vehicles

A **connected vehicle** exchanges data over wireless networks with other vehicles (**V2V**), with roadside equipment such as traffic lights (**V2I**), and with everything around it (**V2X**). A car approaching a junction can be told how many seconds remain before the light turns red. Since 2018, every new model of car approved in the EU has had to include **eCall**, which automatically dials the emergency number 112 after a serious crash and sends the car's location.

@flow Car ahead :: brakes hard in fog > V2V message :: arrives in milliseconds > Car behind :: warns its driver at once

@try
Explain how an adaptive traffic signal uses sensors, a network and software to reduce waiting times compared with a fixed timer. Then suggest one extra penalty you would add to the reward of a traffic light trained with reinforcement learning, and explain why.
@end



@page Self-driving cars and latency

An **autonomous vehicle** drives itself by running the sense–decide–act loop many times every second. **Cameras** read lane markings, signs and traffic lights. **Radar** measures the distance and speed of other vehicles, even in fog or heavy rain. **Lidar** fires laser pulses and times their reflections to build a 3D map of everything nearby. **GPS** and motion sensors track the car's own position. An onboard computer with GPU and AI-accelerator chips combines these readings, decides what to do and sends commands to the **actuators**: an electric motor that turns the steering, and the systems that brake and accelerate. As a car covers many metres every second, *when* a decision arrives matters as much as *what* it is.

@box light | Worked example: speed, latency and braking
@image 5.2 workexamplebraking.PNG w=38 | Hard braking: what matters is how soon it starts.
A car travels at **108 km/h** when a child runs into the road. How far does it travel before the brakes are applied?

**Step 1 — convert the speed.** 108 km/h = 108&nbsp;000 m ÷ 3600 s = **30 m/s**.

**Step 2 — use** distance = speed × delay.
@clear
| Who or what decides | Delay | Distance before braking |
|---|---|---|
| an alert human driver | about 0.67 s | 30 × 0.67 ≈ **20 m** |
| AI over a slow cloud link | 500 ms = 0.500 s | 30 × 0.500 = **15 m** |
| AI over a fast cloud link | 50 ms = 0.050 s | 30 × 0.050 = **1.5 m** |
| AI on the car's own (edge) computer | 5 ms = 0.005 s | 30 × 0.005 = **0.15 m** |

**Conclusion:** deciding on board instead of over the slow cloud link saves 15 − 0.15 = **14.85 m**, more than three car lengths. This is only the distance travelled *before* braking begins; the car then needs extra distance to stop. (The human figure uses the 0.67 s thinking time assumed by the UK Highway Code.)
@end

That is why safety-critical decisions are made at the edge, inside the vehicle, while the cloud handles jobs that can wait: updating maps, collecting data from the whole fleet and training better AI models.

@try
A bus travels at 54 km/h. a) Convert this speed into metres per second. b) Calculate how far it travels before braking begins if the decision takes 300 ms over a cloud link, and if it takes 4 ms on an onboard computer. c) Explain why the bus still needs a network connection.
@end



@page Who is in control?

**Automation** (technology doing tasks with minimal human involvement) comes in levels:

@box dark | Six levels of driving automation (SAE J3016 standard)
| Level | The car … | The human … |
|---|---|---|
| 0 · None | only warns, e.g. of a collision | drives all the time |
| 1 · Assistance | steers *or* controls speed | drives and supervises |
| 2 · Partial | steers *and* controls speed | watches the road constantly |
| 3 · Conditional | drives itself in limited conditions | takes over when asked |
| 4 · High | drives itself within a defined area | is not needed in that area |
| 5 · Full | drives anywhere, in any conditions | is never needed |

Adaptive cruise control is Level 1; driverless taxis are Level 4; no Level 5 car exists yet.
@end

As automation rises, the human shifts from **operator** to **supervisor**: from driving a manual car (traditional **human–computer interaction**, HCI) to supervising a self-driving taxi (**human–AI interaction**, HAI).

@box alt | HCI and HAI compared
| | Traditional HCI | Human–AI interaction |
|---|---|---|
| Behaviour | fixed: same input, same output | adapts to what it senses and learns |
| Human's role | operator giving every command | supervisor setting goals, checking |
| Unclear input | shows an error | makes a best guess, maybe wrong |
@end

HAI goes wrong when people misjudge the machine: with **over-trust** a Level 2 driver stops watching the road, and with **under-trust** a driver switches off a useful safety feature. In 2009, ice blocked the airspeed sensors of Air France Flight 447; the autopilot handed control to pilots who did not understand why, and their confused responses led to a fatal crash. Automation must show clearly what it is doing, and why.

@try
Compare driving a traditional manual car with supervising a self-driving taxi. a) In which situation is the human giving continuous HCI inputs? b) What new responsibilities does the human have in the supervisory HAI role?
@end



@page Robots and automated production
@section 5.5 Industry & Manufacturing

Factories were among the first places to use automation, and today they contain some of the most advanced smart systems. An **industrial robot** is a programmable machine, usually an arm with several joints, that can weld, paint, lift, sort and assemble products with the same precision thousands of times a day. Robots do not get tired or distracted, so a production line can run day and night.

@h2 From a decision to a movement

Inside the robot's computer, a decision is only a number, such as *rotate joint 2 to 90°*. The controller sends this target to a **driver circuit**, which turns it into a control signal (a voltage or PWM pulses) and switches the power the motor needs. The **actuator**, a motor, turns the joint, and an **encoder** measures the new angle and reports it back:

@flow Controller :: target 90° > Driver circuit :: signal and power > Motor :: turns the joint > Encoder :: measures 87°

The controller compares the two angles: 90° − 87° leaves a 3° error, so it drives the motor a little further. This feedback loop runs hundreds of times a second.

@box light | Actuators in a factory
| Actuator | How it works | Typical use |
|---|---|---|
| Electric servo motor | magnetic fields produce precise rotation | robot-arm joints, conveyor belts |
| Solenoid valve | an electromagnet slides a plunger open or shut | dosing liquids, switching air lines |
| Hydraulic piston | pressurised oil produces a very large force | presses, heavy lifting |
| Pneumatic actuator | compressed air gives fast, repeated movement | high-speed pick-and-place grippers |
@end

Automation also changes the kind of mistakes a factory makes. A tired human inspector might miss the odd faulty part; a robot does not get tired. But if a camera is knocked out of line or the software has a bug, a robot repeats the *same* mistake on every product until someone notices, so automated lines still need people to monitor them.

@try
A robot arm is told to rotate a joint to 45°, but its encoder reports 41°. a) Explain what the controller does next. b) Explain why the arm needs both an actuator and a sensor to place parts accurately.
@end



@page Smart factories, learning robots

In a **smart factory**, every machine is networked and sensors stream data to software that watches the whole line. In **predictive maintenance**, the AI notices a motor's vibration rising from 2 mm/s to 6 mm/s over three weeks as its bearings wear, so engineers replace the bearing in a planned stop, not after a breakdown. **Cobots** (collaborative robots) work beside people and stop if they touch someone.

When every object is different, as in a warehouse full of parcels, hand-programming each move is impossible, so engineers use **reinforcement learning**: the robot learns from the results of its actions.

@box light | The learning loop
@image 5.8 reward system.PNG w=31 | A reward is the robot's trophy: a number saying how well an action went.
- **State (*S*):** what the sensors report now, e.g. a camera image of the bin.
- **Action (*A*):** what the robot does: move, rotate, open or close its gripper.
- **Reward (*R*):** positive for success, negative (a **penalty**) for failure.
- **Policy (*π*):** the robot's strategy for choosing actions; learning makes actions that earned high rewards more likely.

In 2018, seven Google robot arms practised grasping; after about 580&nbsp;000 attempts they could pick up objects they had never seen about 96% of the time.
@clear
@end

@box dark | Worked example: the reward decides what "good" means
A warehouse robot moves items into a box. Rewards: item placed **+10**; each second taken **−0.5**; item dropped **−20**; collision **−50**.

**Attempt A** is careful: 12 s, success. Reward = +10 − (0.5 × 12) = **+4**.<br>**Attempt B** is fast: 6 s, but clips a shelf. Reward = +10 − (0.5 × 6) − 50 = **−43**.

Over thousands of attempts the robot learns that careful, collision-free moves pay best; reward speed alone and it would learn to rush and crash. Robots usually practise first in a **simulation**, and safety limits such as emergency stops never depend on the reward.
@end

@try
You are training a behaviourist AI drone to deliver medicine to rural clinics safely. Define a) the sensor data that forms its state, b) one event that earns a positive reward, c) one event that earns a heavy penalty.
@end



@page Smart classrooms
@section 5.6 Education

In education, the three technologies help people learn: they show teachers at once what every student understands, and let students work at their own pace.

@h2 Smart learning devices

- **Tablets and laptops:** touchscreens, cameras and microphones collect every student's work.
- **Interactive whiteboards:** large touch-sensitive displays that detect a pen or finger.
- **Microcontroller kits:** a BBC micro:bit has buttons, a motion sensor, a light sensor and LEDs, so students can build small smart systems of their own.
- **VR headsets:** motion sensors track your head as you explore a virtual volcano.

@h2 Connected classrooms

Devices connect through the school's Wi-Fi to **cloud** learning platforms, where teachers set work, students hand it in and everything is saved automatically. A live quiz shows the network in action:

