The answers travel over Wi-Fi to a cloud server, which counts all 30 in seconds and draws a chart for the teacher. Answer B was wrong, yet 21 ÷ 30 = 70% of the class chose it, so the teacher knows at once to explain the idea again: feedback that would take days with a paper test.
@end

Schools can also become **smart campuses**. In 2021, state schools in England were sent carbon dioxide (CO<sub>2</sub>) monitors that show when a crowded classroom needs fresh air. Occupancy sensors can switch off lights and heating in empty rooms, and software that reads the timetable can warm only the rooms that are about to be used.

@try
Imagine turning your school, college or university into a smart campus. Identify two areas, such as library seating, cafeteria food waste or classroom heating, where sensors, a network and AI software could reduce waste. For one of them, name the sensor, the network and the action taken.
@end



@page Adaptive learning

Behaviourism shaped education long before computers. In the 1950s B. F. Skinner, whose operant conditioning you met in Chapter 2, built **teaching machines** that showed material in small steps and told the student at once whether each answer was right; that instant confirmation acted as reinforcement. **Adaptive learning** uses the same idea, with AI choosing what each learner sees next.

@cycle Ask :: a question > Observe :: right or wrong? > Update :: what you seem to know > Choose :: the next question

Feedback flows both ways: *you* get feedback on each answer, which reinforces correct responses, and the *system* learns whether its question was too easy, too hard or just right.

@box light | Worked example: a levelling rule
A maths app has five levels and one rule: **3 right answers in a row → up one level; 2 wrong in a row → down one level, with a hint.** A student starts on level 2:

| Answers | Results | Level afterwards |
|---|---|---|
| 1–3 | right, right, right | 3 (three right in a row: up) |
| 4–5 | wrong, wrong | 2 (two wrong in a row: down, with a hint) |
| 6 | right | 2 (a new run begins) |

This rule never changes, so it is reactive control. A **learning** system uses thousands of students' results to find which question helps most, rewarded when a learner gets a similar question right a week later.
@end

@box dark | Fairness and privacy
Adaptive systems record every answer and mistake, so schools must protect that data and collect only what they need. Algorithms can also be unfair: in 2020 an algorithm used to award A-level grades in England lowered about 4 in 10 of teachers' predicted grades, and students at large state schools lost out more than those at private schools. After protests, teachers' grades were used instead.
@end

@try
Using the same rule, a student starts on level 3 and answers: right, wrong, right, right, right, wrong, wrong. a) Which level does the student finish on? b) Explain how a learning (reinforcement learning) version of the app would differ from this fixed rule.
@end



@page The smart city: everything together
@section 5.7 Conclusion

Each section of this chapter looked at one setting. A **smart city** joins them all: homes, hospitals, roads, factories and schools share networks and data, so that a whole city can sense what is happening and respond. Thousands of IoT sensors report to AI systems that manage the resources everyone shares: roads, energy, water and clean streets.

@row
@image 5.13 smartcity.PNG | A smart energy grid: solar panels, wind turbines and batteries balanced by networked software.
@box dark | Worked example: a smart grid
On a hot afternoon, air conditioners push electricity demand towards a peak. Smart meters send readings every few minutes over the network to the grid's AI, which predicts the spike and tells large batteries, charged by solar panels during the morning, to release their stored energy. Supply and demand stay balanced without anyone pressing a button.
@end
@endrow

@box light | How a city senses and acts
| City system | Sense → connect → decide → act | Benefit |
|---|---|---|
| Adaptive traffic signals | cameras and road sensors send traffic data over 5G; AI sets the green times | shorter queues, less idling and pollution |
| Smart water network | acoustic sensors on water mains hear small leaks; valves close to isolate the pipe | less clean water lost, cheaper repairs |
| Smart street lighting | motion sensors dim lights on empty streets and brighten them as people approach | lower energy use |
| Smart waste collection | bins report how full they are; software plans the lorries' routes | fewer trips, less fuel and traffic |
@end

Smart cities show both sides of this chapter at once. They can save energy, water, time and lives, but they depend on huge amounts of data about where people go and what they do, and a single attack on their networks could affect a whole city.

@try
Choose one city system from the table. Name its sensor, its network and its actuator, and explain what the AI decides. Then suggest one way the system could fail and how the city could reduce that risk.
@end



@page Opportunities and responsibilities

Every application in this chapter brings benefits and new risks. Once, security teams protected only servers and desktop computers; now every connected lock, camera, sensor and medical pump is another way in for an attacker, and many cheap devices ship with weak passwords. Smart systems also collect data constantly, about where people go and how they live, which can be stolen, sold or misused.

@box light | One technology, two sides
| Technology | Opportunity | Security or privacy risk |
|---|---|---|
| Health monitors | early warning of emergencies | stolen records; altered doses |
| Home cameras | intruder alerts from anywhere | hijacked video feeds |
| Delivery robots | fast deliveries, lower emissions | GPS spoofing misdirects them |
| City sensors | less wasted energy and water | tracking where everyone goes |
@end

Beyond security lies a second question: are the decisions right? **Ethical AI** means developing and using AI in ways that are fair, responsible and transparent:

@box dark | The three pillars of ethical AI
| Pillar | What it means | Why it is hard |
|---|---|---|
| Fairness | people and groups are treated equally | AI repeats bias hidden in past data, as the 2020 grading algorithm showed |
| Transparency | people can find out how a decision was made | many AI systems are black boxes |
| Accountability | someone is clearly responsible when things go wrong | a hardware maker, software developer, network provider and user all play a part |

The law is beginning to answer the accountability question. Under the UK's Automated Vehicles Act 2024, while an approved self-driving system is driving, the company behind it is responsible, not the person in the seat.
@end

@try
A city wants cameras at every bus stop to count waiting passengers and send extra buses. Give one opportunity and one privacy risk, then suggest one way to make the plan fairer or more transparent.
@end



@page Shaping everyday life

Across every setting in this chapter the pattern is the same. **Hardware** senses the world and changes it, **networks** carry information between devices, people and the cloud, and **behaviourist AI** chooses actions and improves them from feedback. Remove any one and the system fails: without sensors it is blind, without a network it is cut off, and without intelligent software it cannot adapt.

@box light | One pattern, five settings
| Setting | Hardware | Network | Behaviourist AI |
|---|---|---|---|
| Smart home | thermostat, relay | Wi-Fi, Zigbee | learns your heating times |
| Healthcare | glucose sensor, insulin pump | Bluetooth | adjusts doses from feedback |
| Transportation | cameras, radar, brakes | V2V, V2I, 5G | signals rewarded for short queues |
| Industry | robot arms, encoders | factory network | robots learn to grasp |
| Education | tablets, whiteboards | Wi-Fi, cloud | picks the next question |
@end

Think about a single day: the thermostat warms the house before you wake, adaptive signals speed up your bus, a live quiz shows your teacher what to explain again, and your watch tracks your sleep. Each time, hardware senses, a network connects and software decides, usually without you noticing:

@flow 07:00 :: a warm house > 08:15 :: green lights > 10:30 :: a live quiz > 23:00 :: sleep tracking

@h2 Future innovations

Faster networks, cheaper sensors and more efficient AI chips will move more intelligence to the edge, close to where things happen. Researchers are already building prosthetic hands that adapt to the way their wearer moves, farm robots that weed a field plant by plant instead of spraying it all, and power grids that balance wind and solar energy automatically. Each of these is a feedback loop of sensing, connecting, deciding and acting. However clever the machines become, people still choose their goals and the rewards they learn from, so understanding all three technologies, and their limits, matters for everyone. You can try the loop yourself in **RoverLab**: build a machine, connect its network and train its behaviour.

@try
Pick the application from this chapter that you would trust least. Using fairness, transparency and accountability, explain what would need to change before you would trust it.
@end



@page Practice
@label Chapter 5 · Practice
@plane none

@practice light | Practice · Chapter 5
1. Define a smart system and explain how it integrates physical hardware, network connectivity and software into a unified workflow.
2. Real-time processing and automation
    1. Define real-time processing and explain why low network latency and high-speed hardware are critical for systems like autonomous vehicle collision avoidance.
    2. An autonomous car travels at 90 km/h. Calculate how far it travels before braking begins if the decision takes 400 ms over a cloud connection, and if it takes 8 ms on an onboard computer. Show your working.
    3. What is automation, and how does moving from human-operated control to fully automated smart systems alter operational efficiency and error rates?
3. Feedback and action
    1. Explain how a behaviourist AI system uses performance feedback or reward signals from its environment to modify its behavioural policy.
    2. Describe the role of actuators in translating an AI model's computed decision into physical changes within the real world.
4. Humans and AI
    1. Define human-AI interaction (HAI) and explain how it differs from traditional human-computer interaction (HCI).
    2. Identify a major usability or safety challenge that occurs when a smart system's autonomous behaviour lacks transparency, leaving the human user confused about its next action.
    3. An AI system alerts a doctor: "High risk of patient sepsis – transfer to ICU immediately." Suggest how the alert screen could be redesigned so that the doctor can check it within 5 seconds, listing three pieces of evidence it should display.
5. Ethics and society
    1. Define ethical AI and discuss why transparency, fairness and accountability are difficult to maintain in complex smart systems.
    2. Identify one major opportunity (e.g. resource optimisation in smart cities) and one major security or privacy risk associated with connecting everyday physical devices to public networks.
@end



@page Practice
@label Chapter 5 · Practice
@plane none

@practice light | Practice · Chapter 5 (continued)
6. Smart homes. A family installs a learning thermostat, a smart door lock and a security camera.
    1. For each device, name one sensor and one actuator or output that it uses.
    2. Explain how the learning thermostat changes its schedule using feedback from the family.
    3. Describe two steps the family should take to protect these devices from attackers.
7. Healthcare
    1. Describe how a smartwatch and a mobile phone could help a doctor monitor a patient with a heart condition at home. Name the sensors and networks involved.
    2. Explain why a hybrid closed-loop insulin system is an example of a system that adapts to feedback.
8. Transportation
    1. Explain how vehicle-to-vehicle (V2V) communication could prevent a crash in fog.
    2. Describe the difference between Level 2 and Level 4 driving automation, including what the human must do at each level.
9. Industry and manufacturing. A warehouse robot is trained with reinforcement learning to move parcels onto a conveyor belt.
    1. Suggest a suitable state, action, reward and penalty for the robot.
    2. Using a reward table of your own, show how the robot's total reward would differ for a slow, safe move and a fast move that hits a shelf.
    3. Explain what could go wrong if the robot were rewarded for speed only.
10. Education
    1. Explain how an adaptive learning app uses a student's answers as feedback to choose the next question.
    2. Give one fairness concern and one privacy concern about using AI systems in schools.
11. Using examples from at least two settings in this chapter, evaluate how hardware, networks and behaviourist AI combine to shape everyday life.
@end
